<section xml:id="sec-flow-if">
	<title>Conditional Flow</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			So far, we've written code that runs steps in sequence. But real programs 
			often need to make decisions, choosing different actions based on conditions. 
			This is called flow control. In MATLAB, you use things like <pf>if</pf>-statements or 
			<pf>switch</pf>-statements to guide your code down the right path.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-if-single-gate">
		<title><pf>if</pf> Gate</title>

		<p>
			The simplest <pf>if</pf>-statement has one condition and one block of code. The code runs only when the
			condition is true.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="single-if-gate-structure">
			<title><pf>if</pf> Gate Structure</title>

				<p>
					Use Case: 
					<ul>
						<li>Perform <pf>taskA</pf> if <pf>logicalStatementA</pf> is <pf>true</pf>.</li>
					</ul>
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					if logicalStatementA
						%		⤷ if true  ➜ do taskA ➜ end
						%		⤷ if false ➜ end
						taskA
					end
				</program>

		</assemblage>

		<p>
			To see how this works, run the following code block in the MATLAB <m>\texttt{cwd}</m>:
		</p>

		<sage language="octave">
			<input>
				age = 23;

				if age &gt;= 21
					fprintf("Bouncer: You may enter the bar.\n");
				end
			</input>
		</sage>

		<p>
			Since <pf>age</pf> is <pf>23</pf>, the logical statement <pf>age &gt;= 21</pf> 
			is true, so MATLAB executes the <pf>fprintf</pf> line and prints the message. 
			Now try changing <pf>age</pf> to <pf>16</pf> and running it again. This time, 
			the <pf>age &gt;= 21</pf> is false, so MATLAB skips the <pf>fprintf</pf> line 
			and nothing is printed.
		</p>

	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-if-else-gate">
		<title><pf>if-else</pf> Gate</title>

		<p>
			When you have more than one possibility, you can add an <pf>else</pf> branch. MATLAB checks the <pf>if</pf> condition;
			if it is true, it executes the <pf>if</pf> branch; if it is false, it moves directly into the <pf>else</pf> branch.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="if-else-gate-structure">
			<title><pf>if-else</pf> Gate Structure</title>

				<p>
					Use Case: 
					<ul>
						<li>Perform <pf>taskA</pf> if <pf>logicalStatementA</pf> is <pf>true</pf>,</li>
						<li>otherwise perform <pf>taskB</pf>.</li>
					</ul>
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					if logicalStatementA
						%		⤷ if true  ➜ do taskA ➜ end
						%		⤷ if false ➜ do taskB ➜ end
						taskA
					else
						taskB
					end
				</program>

		</assemblage>

		<p>
			Building on the previous example, here is a code block that uses an <pf>if-else</pf> statement:
		</p>

		<sage language="octave">
			<input>
				age = 23;

				if age &gt;= 21
					fprintf("Bouncer: You may enter the bar.\n");
				else
					fprintf("Bouncer: Move along kid.\n");
				end
			</input>
		</sage>

		<p>
			Since <pf>age</pf> is <pf>23</pf>, the logical statement <pf>age &gt;= 21</pf> is true, so MATLAB prints the first message. Now try changing <pf>age</pf> to <pf>16</pf> and running it again. This time, the logical statement is false, so MATLAB tells you to move along.
		</p>

	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-if-elseif-else-gate">
		<title><pf>if-elseif-else</pf> Gate</title>

		<p>
			An <pf>else</pf> branch acts as a fallback: it runs only if every earlier condition is false.
			This is useful when you want to guarantee that some variable gets assigned a value.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="if-elseif-else-gate-structure">
			<title><pf>if-elseif-else</pf> Gate Structure</title>

				<p>
					Use Case: 
					<ul>
						<li>Perform <pf>taskA</pf> if <pf>logicalStatementA</pf> is <pf>true</pf>,</li>
						<li>Perform <pf>taskB</pf> if <pf>logicalStatementB</pf> is <pf>true</pf>,</li>
						<li>Perform <pf>taskC</pf> if <pf>logicalStatementC</pf> is <pf>true</pf>,</li>
						<li>otherwise perform <pf>taskD</pf> (default).</li>
					</ul>
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					if logicalStatementA
						%		⤷ if true  ➜ do taskA ➜ end
						%		⤷ if false ➜ check logicalStatementB
						taskA
					elseif logicalStatementB
						%		⤷ if true  ➜ do taskB ➜ end
						%		⤷ if false ➜ check logicalStatementC
						taskB
					elseif logicalStatementC
						%		⤷ if true  ➜ do taskC ➜ end
						%		⤷ if false ➜ do default taskD ➜ end
						taskC
					else
						taskD
					end
				</program>

				<p>
					Notes:
					<ul>
						<li>You can add as many <pf>elseif</pf> branches as needed.</li>
						<li>The <pf>else</pf> (default) branch is optional.</li>
						<li>The conditions are checked in order from top to bottom.</li>
						<li>Only one of the tasks will be performed when the code runs.</li>
				<li>
					Every <pf>if</pf>-statement must end with <pf>end</pf>.
				</li>
				<li>
					Each <pf>logicalStatement</pf> must evaluate to <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.
				</li>
				<li>
					The <pf>else</pf> block runs only when all preceding conditions are false.
				</li>
				<li>
					You can nest <pf>if</pf>-statements within other <pf>if</pf>-statements, but this should be avoided whenever possible.
				</li>
					</ul>
				</p>

		</assemblage>

		<sage language="octave">
			<input>
				x = 3;
				y = 2;

				if y ~= 3
					z = 4;
				elseif x &lt; y
					z = 3;
				else
					z = 0;
				end

				fprintf("The value of z is: %d\n", z);
			</input>
		</sage>

		<p>
			For practice, test this block with different initial values such as
			<pf>x = 0; y = 4;</pf>, <pf>x = 0; y = 3;</pf>, and <pf>x = 3; y = 3;</pf>.
			Watch how only one branch executes each time.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-switch-gates">
		<title><pf>switch</pf> Gate</title>
		
		<p>
			The switch statement is another option for controlling code in MATLAB. It works similarly to the if-statement by directing your code to go down one of many possible paths.
		</p>

		<p>
			The switch statement determines which task to perform based on possible values of some variable. The conditions we use with switch statements are in the form of cases. 
		</p>

		<aside>
			<p>
				Like the if-statement, you can have as many branches as you like. You would just add additional <pf>case</pf> statements between the <pf>switch</pf> and <pf>otherwise</pf> statements.
			</p>
		</aside>

		<assemblage xml:id="switch-statement-def">
			<title><pf>switch</pf> Gate Structure</title>

			<p>
				Use Case: 
				<ul>
					<li>Perform <pf>taskA</pf> if <pf>varName</pf> is equal to <pf>val1</pf>,</li>
					<li>Perform <pf>taskB</pf> if <pf>varName</pf> is equal to <pf>val2</pf> or <pf>val3</pf>,</li>
					<li>Perform <pf>taskC</pf> if <pf>varName</pf> is equal to <pf>val4</pf>,</li>
					<li>otherwise perform <pf>taskD</pf> (default).</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
				switch varName

					case val1
						%  ⤷ varName == val1?  YES ➜ do taskA ➜ end
						%                       NO ➜ next case
						taskA

					case {val2, val3}
						%   ⤷     ⤷ varName == val2 OR val3?  YES ➜ do taskB ➜ end
						%                                      NO ➜ next case
						taskB

					case val4
						%  ⤷ varName == val4?  YES ➜ do taskC ➜ end
						%                       NO ➜ do taskD ➜ end
						taskC

					otherwise
						taskD

				end
			</program>

			<p>
				Rules:
				<ul>
					<li>Case values are checked in order from top to bottom.</li>
					<li>The <pf>otherwise</pf> block runs only when no cases match.</li>
					<li>The <pf>otherwise</pf> (default) branch is optional.</li>
					<li>When there is only one case value, curly braces <pf>{ }</pf> are optional.</li>
					<li>Every <pf>switch</pf>-statement must end with <pf>end</pf>.</li>
					<li>Case values can be numbers, strings, or other data types that support equality comparisons.</li>
					<li>Case values are case-sensitive when comparing strings.</li>
					<li>Case values must be unique; duplicate case values will cause an error.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>
				Limitations:
				<ul>
					<li>Case values cannot be variables. They are known hard-coded values.</li>
					<li>Case values cannot use logical operators (e.g., <pf>&lt;</pf>, <pf>&gt;</pf>, <pf>==</pf>, <pf>&amp;</pf>, <pf>|</pf>).</li>
					<li>Switch statements are not suitable for range-based conditions (e.g., checking if a value is between two numbers).</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<p>
			The <pf>switch</pf>-<pf>case</pf> structure is similar to the <pf>if</pf> structure, but offers a few advantages.	First, it is easier to read; second, it is better when comparing strings (of possibly different lengths). The basic format is:
		</p>

		<p>
			Let's check whether a cadet is in their first two years at VMI.
		</p>

		<sage language="octave">
			<input>
				year = 'second class';

				switch year
					case{'fourth class', 'third class'}
						fprintf("You are in the first two years.\n");
					case{'second class', 'first class'}
						fprintf("You are in the last two years.\n");
					otherwise
						fprintf("You must be a fifth year.\n");
				end
			</input>
		</sage>

		<p>
			The cases are grouped by curly brackets so that a case will be satisfied if the value of Year is any of the values in a specific case. Once this code is executed, the switch command will look at the value of Year, and the output should be
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			<code>
				You are in your last two years.
			</code>
		</program>

	</subsection>

	<reading-questions>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				The examples below emphasize two goals: translating English conditions into logical statements, and writing
				conditions that are readable.
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<exercise xml:id="if-activity-1">
			<title>Example 1: Simple <pf>if</pf>-gate</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Complete the following tasks.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a code block that defines <pf>x</pf> as <pf>6</pf> and sets <pf>y</pf> according to the following rule:
						<ul marker="">
							<li>if <pf>x</pf> is less than or equal to 9, set <pf>y</pf> to 1, otherwise set <pf>y</pf> to 4.</li>
						</ul>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = 6;

						if x &lt;= 9
							y = 1;
						else
							y = 4;
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
				<solution>
					<title>(with commentary)</title>

					<program language="matlab">
						x = 6;

						if x &lt;= 9
							fprintf('x = %d ≤ 9 is true\n', x);
							y = 1;
						else
							fprintf('x = %d ≤ 9 is false\n', x);
							y = 4;
						end

						fprintf('so we set y to %d\n', y);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Change <pf>x</pf> to <pf>16</pf> and rerun your code block.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>Just change the value of <pf>x</pf> to <pf>16</pf> in the previous code block.</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Now, suppose we define a variable <pf>x</pf> as a random integer between -5 and 15:
					</p>
					<program>
						x = randi([-5, 15]);
					</program>
					<p>
						Rerun your code block multiple times to see how the value of <pf>y</pf> changes based on the random value of <pf>x</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>Just change the value of <pf>x</pf> to <pf>randi([-5, 15])</pf> in the previous code block.</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write a code block that sets <pf>w</pf> according to the following rules:
						<ul>
							<li>if <pf>x</pf> is equal to 9, set <pf>w</pf> to 1</li>
							<li>if <pf>x</pf> is between (but not equal to) -4 and 0, set <pf>w</pf> to 2</li>
							<li>if <pf>x</pf> is not less than 3 or greater than 9, set <pf>w</pf> to 3</li>
							<li>if none of the above are true, set <pf>w</pf> to 4</li>
						</ul>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = randi([-5, 15]);

						if x == 9
							w = 1;
						elseif x &gt; -4 &amp;&amp; x &lt; 0
							w = 2;
						elseif ~(x &lt;= 3) || x &gt; 9
							w = 3;
						else
							w = 4;
						end
					</program>
				</solution>
				<solution>
					<title>
						(with commentary)
					</title>
					
					<program language="matlab">
						x = randi([-5, 15]);
						fprintf('x = %d\n', x);

						if x == 9
							fprintf('x == 9 is true\n');
							w = 1;
						elseif x &gt; -4 &amp;&amp; x &lt; 0
							fprintf('-4 &lt; x &lt; 0 is true\n');
							w = 2;
						elseif ~(x &lt;= 3) || x &gt; 9
							fprintf('x is not less than or equal to 3 or greater than 9 is true\n');
							w = 3;
						else
							fprintf('none of the above conditions are true\n');
							w = 4;
						end

						fprintf('so we set w to %d\n', w);
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>



	<exercise xml:id="f-activity-4">
		<title><pf>if-else</pf></title>

		<statement>
			<p>
				Suppose <pf>num</pf> is a <pf>1 x 1 double</pf>. Write a code block that increases <pf>num</pf> by 5 if it is greater than 10 and decreases it by 5 otherwise.
			</p>
		</statement>

		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				<code>
					num = 68;
					if num > 10
						num = num + 5;
					else
						num = num - 5;
					end
				</code>
			</program>
		</solution>

		<solution>
			<title>With Commentary</title>

			<program language="matlab">
				<code>
					num = 68;
					fprintf("Since num = " + num + ", we ")
					if num > 10
						num = num + 5;
						fprintf("add 5 to get num = " + num + "\n")
					else
						num = num - 5;
						fprintf("subtract 5 to get num = " + num + "\n")
					end
				</code>
			</program>

		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="f-activity-5">
		<title>Optional <pf>else</pf></title>

		<statement>
			<p>
				Suppose <pf>num</pf> is a <pf>1 x 1 double</pf>. Write a code block that increases <pf>num</pf> by 5 if it is greater than 10 and decreases it by 5 otherwise.
			</p>
			<p>
				Suppose <pf>x</pf> contains a numeric value. Write a script that increases <pf>x</pf> by 5 if it is greater than 10, decreases <pf>x</pf> by 5 if it is less than 10, and does nothing if <pf>x</pf> is zero.
			</p>
		</statement>

		<solution>
			<p>
				If <pf>x = 0</pf>, the below script runs the commands <pf>x = x - 5</pf> and <pf>disp(x)</pf>, outputting <pf>-5</pf>. However, if <pf>x = 10</pf>, only the <pf>disp(x)</pf> command is run, producing an output of <pf>10</pf>.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
				<code>
					if x &gt; 10
						x = x + 5;
					elseif x &lt; 10
						x = x - 5;
					end
					disp(x)
				</code>
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="sec-quadratic-equation">
	<title>Case Study: Solving the Quadratic Equation in MATLAB</title>

	<introduction>
	<p>
		A classic programming task is turning a familiar mathematical procedure into a reliable piece of code.
		The quadratic equation is a great example because it combines algebra, branching logic, and careful handling
		of special cases.
	</p>

	<p>
		Consider the quadratic polynomial <m>a x^2 + b x + c</m>. Its roots satisfy
		<me>x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.</me>
		The expression <m>D = b^2 - 4ac</m> is called the <term>discriminant</term>, and its sign determines
		the type of roots.
	</p>
	</introduction>


	<task xml:id="subsec-quadratic-discriminant">
		<title>The Discriminant and Root Types</title>

		<p>
			There are three common cases:
			if <m>D &gt; 0</m> there are two distinct real roots, if <m>D = 0</m> there is one repeated real root,
			and if <m>D &lt; 0</m> the roots are complex conjugates.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
a = 1; b = -3; c = 2;        % (x-1)(x-2) = 0

D = b^2 - 4*a*c;

x1 = (-b + sqrt(D)) / (2*a);
x2 = (-b - sqrt(D)) / (2*a);
		</program>

		<p>
			MATLAB’s <code>sqrt</code> can produce complex results automatically when <m>D &lt; 0</m>,
			so the computation above still works. But it is often useful to detect the case and present results clearly.
		</p>
	</task>

	<task xml:id="subsec-quadratic-function">
		<title>Packaging the Computation as a Function</title>

		<p>
			A function is the natural way to turn this idea into reusable code.
			We also add input checks to catch the invalid case <m>a = 0</m>, which would not be a quadratic.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
function [r1, r2] = quadraticRoots(a,b,c)
%QUADRATICROOTS Return the roots of a*x^2 + b*x + c.
%
%   r1, r2 are the two roots (possibly equal or complex).

		if a == 0
				error("Input a must be nonzero for a quadratic polynomial.");
		end

		D = b^2 - 4*a*c;
		r1 = (-b + sqrt(D)) / (2*a);
		r2 = (-b - sqrt(D)) / (2*a);
end
		</program>

		<p>
			A function like this becomes more valuable when paired with clear documentation and predictable output format.
			In practice, you might also return the discriminant or a label indicating the root type.
		</p>
	</task>

	<task xml:id="subsec-quadratic-numerics">
		<title>A Numerical Note</title>

		<p>
			For some coefficient values, the quadratic formula can suffer from roundoff error.
			This usually appears when <m>b^2</m> is much larger than <m>4ac</m>, causing subtractive cancellation.
			For introductory programming, the standard formula is perfectly acceptable, but it is good to know that
			numerical stability can matter even in “simple” algebra.
		</p>
	</task>
</exercise>

<!-- demo_if_statements.m -->
<exercise xml:id="sec-if-statements">
	<title>Making Decisions with if Statements</title>

	<introduction>
	<p>
		An <code>if</code> statement lets your program choose what to do based on a logical condition.
		This is the core mechanism behind “decision-making” in code: the program tests a condition and follows
		the branch that matches the result.
	</p>
	</introduction>


	<task xml:id="subsec-if-basic-syntax">
		<title>Basic if Syntax</title>

		<p>
			The simplest form has one condition and one block of code.
			If the condition is true, MATLAB executes the block. Otherwise, MATLAB skips it.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
x = -2;

if x &lt; 0
		disp("x is negative");
end
		</program>

		<p>
			The condition must evaluate to a single logical value.
			If you accidentally supply a logical array, MATLAB will error in modern versions.
			In those cases you typically want <code>any</code> or <code>all</code>.
		</p>
	</task>

	<task xml:id="subsec-if-elseif-else">
		<title>Multiple Branches with elseif and else</title>

		<p>
			Use <code>elseif</code> to test additional conditions, and <code>else</code> as a catch-all when none match.
			MATLAB checks conditions in order and takes the first branch that is true.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
score = 83;

if score &gt;= 90
		letter = "A";
elseif score &gt;= 80
		letter = "B";
elseif score &gt;= 70
		letter = "C";
elseif score &gt;= 60
		letter = "D";
else
		letter = "F";
end

disp("Letter grade: " + letter)
		</program>
	</task>

	<task xml:id="subsec-if-logical-operators">
		<title>Using Logical Operators in Conditions</title>

		<p>
			Many conditions combine multiple requirements. For scalar logic, prefer <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code>.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
x = 4;

if (x &gt; 0) &amp;&amp; (x &lt; 10)
		disp("x is between 0 and 10");
end
		</program>

	</task>

	<task xml:id="subsec-if-best-practices">
		<title>Common Pitfalls and Best Practices</title>

		<p>
			Two habits prevent a lot of bugs:
			first, make your conditions explicit and readable; second, test edge cases (like zeros, empty arrays,
			or unexpected input types).
		</p>

		<p>
			For text comparisons, use functions like <code>strcmp</code> rather than <code>==</code>
			(especially when working with character arrays).
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
reply = "yes";

if strcmpi(reply, "yes")
		disp("Continuing...");
else
		disp("Stopping...");
end
		</program>

<paragraphs xml:id="subsubsec-short-circuit-operators">
	<title>Short-Circuit Operators: <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf> and <pf>||</pf></title>

	<p>
		MATLAB also provides <term>short-circuit</term> logical operators: <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf> (AND) and <pf>||</pf> (OR).
		These work only with scalar logical values (true/false), not arrays.
		When the left side of the expression already determines the outcome, MATLAB skips evaluating the right side.
	</p>


	<program language="matlab">
		x = 4;

		% Scalar logic (typical in conditionals)
		(x &gt; 0) &amp;&amp; (x &lt; 10)

	</program>

</paragraphs>

	</task>


</exercise>

